What is Object Oriented Programming?
	Object-Oriented Programming (OOP) is a programming paradigm that organizes software around objects rather than functions. An object represents a real-world entity and contains both data (fields/attributes) and behavior (methods). OOP helps make code modular, reusable, maintainable, and scalable through four main principles: Encapsulation, Inheritance, Polymorphism, and Abstraction.
		Advantages of OOP 
			Code Reusability
			Better Code Organization
			Easy Maintenance
			Easy Scalability
			Better Security (Encapsulation)
			Real-world Modeling
			
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

What is a class?
	A class is a blueprint or template used to create objects. It defines the properties (fields/variables) and behaviors (methods) that the objects of that class will have. A class itself does not occupy memory for object data until an object is created.

Characteristics of a Class
	It is a user-defined data type.
	It acts as a blueprint for creating objects.
	It contains:
		Fields (variables)
		Methods
		Constructors
		Blocks (optional)
		Nested classes (optional)
	It does not store object-specific data until objects are created.
	
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

What is an object?
	An object is an instance of a class. It is a real entity created from a class that contains its own copy of the class's fields (data) and can use the class's methods (behavior). Objects occupy memory when they are created using the new keyword.
	
Characteristics of an Object
	Every object has three characteristics:
		1. State
			The data stored in the object.
		2. Behavior
			The actions the object can perform.
		3. Identity
			A unique identity assigned by the JVM, which distinguishes one object from another, even if they contain the same data.
			
Objects are stored in heap memory.
The reference variable (v1) is stored in the stack memory.

An object is an instance of a class. It is created using the new keyword and contains its own copy of the class's fields and can access the class's methods. Objects represent real-world entities, occupy memory in the heap, and each object has its own state, behavior, and identity.

