Methods
	A method is a block of code inside a class that defines the behavior or actions of an object. It performs a specific task and can be called whenever needed, making code reusable and organized.
	
Components of a Method
	public int add(int a, int b) {
	    return a + b;
	}
	
	Part	Meaning
	public	Access modifier
	int	Return type
	add	Method name
	(int a, int b)	Parameters
	return	Returns the result
	
Types of Methods
	1. Method Without Parameters
		void greet() {
		    System.out.println("Hello");
		}
	2. Method With Parameters
		void greet(String name) {
		    System.out.println("Hello " + name);
		}
	3. Method With Return Value
		int add(int a, int b) {
		    return a + b;
		}
	4. Method Without Return Value
		void printName() {
		    System.out.println("Rahul");
		}
		
Instance Method vs Static Method
	Instance Method
		Belongs to an object.
			class Student {

			    void study() {
				System.out.println("Study");
			    }
			}
		Call:
			Student s = new Student();
			s.study();
	Static Method
		Belongs to the class.
			class Student {
			    static void collegeName() {
				System.out.println("ABC College");
			    }
			}
		Call:
			Student.collegeName();
			
A method is a block of code defined inside a class that represents the behavior of an object. It performs a specific task and can accept parameters and return a value. Methods improve code reusability, readability, and maintainability.
